Endocrine & Metabolic Neuropathic Syndromes (Diabetes Mellitus Chronic Kidney Disease Hypothyroid-Associated deficiencies B1/B6/B12/E)
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Symmetrical stocking-glove sensory loss, Burning nocturnal distal pain, Absent Achilles reflexes

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-011
Reason for Visit: Unmanageable acute exacerbation of severe nocturnal lower limb pain preventing sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented severe, unremitting burning pain in the lower extremities preventing sleep and causing extreme distress. Urgent in-person evaluation is required to rule out acute limb ischemia and provide immediate neuropathic analgesia before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet are burning like they are in a fire, and shooting pains keep waking me up. I haven't slept in three nights."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of bilateral, symmetric, and progressive stocking-and-glove distribution of distal sensory impairment. Over the past 72 hours, there has been an acute exacerbation of burning, lancinating, or electrical pain character predominantly affecting the toes and ascending the lower extremities. There is a profound nocturnal exacerbation of burning pain that frequently and severely disrupts sleep. The patient notes diminished or completely absent vibratory and position sense in the distal extremities, along with progressive loss of protective sensation, resulting in unperceived minor injuries. The patient reports episodic lightheadedness upon standing, suggesting autonomic neuropathy causing orthostatic hypotension. There is variable distal motor weakness developing long after the onset of distinct sensory signs, and trophic skin changes including dry, cracked, anhidrotic skin and localized hair loss on the distal limbs.
Functional Status: Independent mobility, but significantly impaired by severe neuropathic pain and sleep deprivation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of falls secondary to sensory ataxia and unperceived foot injuries.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 150/90 mmHg (supine), 130/75 mmHg (standing) | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Fatigued, distressed, frequently shifting leg position due to severe discomfort.
Physical Exam: Lower extremity examination reveals symmetrical stocking-glove sensory loss to pinprick, temperature, and 10g monofilament testing. There is early loss or complete absence of the Achilles deep tendon reflexes bilaterally. The feet exhibit dry, anhidrotic skin with minor fissuring on the heels. Bilateral dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are palpable and bounding. No focal motor deficits proximally; mild weakness in toe extension bilaterally. Capillary refill is brisk.
Environmental Observations: Scattered rugs pose a tripping hazard; poorly lit hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Metformin 1000mg BD; over-the-counter paracetamol providing zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Limb Ischemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bounding peripheral pulses, warm extremities, and brisk capillary refill.

Lumbar Spinal Stenosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symptoms are predominantly nocturnal and at rest, rather than induced by standing or walking (neurogenic claudication).

Complex Regional Pain Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical presentation without profound localized edema or vasomotor asymmetry.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of painful diabetic peripheral neuropathy resulting in agonizing insomnia and functional decline. The clinical presentation is classic for small-fiber neuropathic pain, which is notoriously refractory to simple analgesics. Delaying clinical assessment until the morning would subject the patient to prolonged, excruciating pain and exhaustion, increasing the immediate risk of a severe fall due to sensory ataxia and orthostatic hypotension. Urgent after-hours assessment allowed for the definitive exclusion of vascular emergencies and the immediate initiation of targeted, membrane-stabilizing neuropathic agents, directly fulfilling MBS criteria for an urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Amitriptyline 10mg orally at night. Prescribed a short course of Tapentadol 50mg PRN for severe breakthrough pain overnight. Arranged urgent podiatry referral.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Step 1: By applying a thick, unmedicated emollient to the heels every night before bed, the patient actively restores skin moisture, immediately preventing deep, painful cracks that can become infected.

Step 2: By deliberately standing up slowly and waiting 30 seconds before walking, the patient allows their blood pressure to stabilize, drastically reducing the risk of a fall from orthostatic hypotension.

Step 3: By wearing well-fitted, protective slippers indoors at all times, the patient provides a physical barrier against unnoticed trauma, entirely preventing accidental puncture wounds to the numb soles.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Podiatry home visit.
DME/Safety (Optional): Removal of loose floor rugs.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to titrate Amitriptyline and review glycemic control.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department if the foot becomes entirely cold, turns pale or blue, or if you develop a new, rapidly spreading red and hot area on the skin.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Neuropathic pain requires slow medication titration; complete relief takes time.
Clinical Course: The burning nerve pain is expected to lessen gradually over the next 1-2 weeks as the medication reaches therapeutic levels. Sleep should improve within 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent review if the pain completely fails to respond, or if you notice any new, painless open sores on your feet.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The high blood sugar has damaged the small nerves in your feet, causing them to misfire and send false pain signals, especially at night. Normal painkillers do not work for this. The new medication works by calming these hyperactive nerves so you can sleep.
Actionable Steps: Take the Amitriptyline 2 hours before bed. Wear slippers constantly. Stand up slowly to prevent dizziness.

